1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,730
Listen, practice, speak. Practice makes you perfect.

2
00:00:13,730 --> 00:00:17,370
Hello and welcome to Business English Conversations.

3
00:00:17,370 --> 00:00:21,610
Hi everyone, I'm Erica and we're so happy you're here with us today.

4
00:00:21,610 --> 00:00:22,610
And I'm Daniel.

5
00:00:22,610 --> 00:00:24,330
Thank you so much for joining us.

6
00:00:24,330 --> 00:00:28,810
On this podcast, we help you practice real business English through natural professional

7
00:00:28,810 --> 00:00:30,170
conversations.

8
00:00:30,170 --> 00:00:34,490
If you enjoy learning with us, make sure to follow our channel and share this episode

9
00:00:34,490 --> 00:00:38,870
with a colleague or friend who wants to improve their English at work.

10
00:00:38,870 --> 00:00:43,670
Your support helps us create more practical content for professionals like you.

11
00:00:43,670 --> 00:00:47,190
Today we have a very important and practical topic.

12
00:00:47,190 --> 00:00:48,190
That's right.

13
00:00:48,190 --> 00:00:52,190
Today we're going to talk about how to introduce yourself in a business meeting.

14
00:00:52,190 --> 00:00:56,310
This might sound simple, but it's actually one of the most powerful communication

15
00:00:56,310 --> 00:00:58,830
skills in your professional life.

16
00:00:58,830 --> 00:01:00,070
Absolutely.

17
00:01:00,070 --> 00:01:04,350
The way you introduce yourself can shape people's first impression of you.

18
00:01:04,350 --> 00:01:11,070
And in business, first impressions can influence trust, credibility, and even future opportunities.

19
00:01:11,070 --> 00:01:14,950
So today, we'll walk you through different situations where you may need to introduce

20
00:01:14,950 --> 00:01:15,950
yourself.

21
00:01:15,950 --> 00:01:17,830
We'll talk about structure.

22
00:01:17,830 --> 00:01:19,710
We'll share useful phrases.

23
00:01:19,710 --> 00:01:22,030
We'll explain key vocabulary.

24
00:01:22,030 --> 00:01:26,830
We'll practice real examples so you can feel confident the next time you're in a meeting.

25
00:01:26,830 --> 00:01:29,270
So Daniel, let me ask you something.

26
00:01:29,270 --> 00:01:33,670
Do you remember the first time you had to introduce yourself in an important business

27
00:01:33,670 --> 00:01:36,470
Oh yes, very clearly.

28
00:01:36,470 --> 00:01:40,910
I had just joined a new company and on my first day I had to introduce myself to the

29
00:01:40,910 --> 00:01:42,630
entire department.

30
00:01:42,630 --> 00:01:44,190
That sounds stressful.

31
00:01:44,190 --> 00:01:45,190
It was.

32
00:01:45,190 --> 00:01:49,470
I was confident about my experience, but I wasn't sure how much detail to give.

33
00:01:49,470 --> 00:01:53,230
I didn't want to talk too much, but I also didn't want to sound unprepared.

34
00:01:53,230 --> 00:01:56,070
That's a very common problem.

35
00:01:56,070 --> 00:02:01,270
Many professionals ask the same question, how long should my introduction be?

36
00:02:01,270 --> 00:02:02,270
Exactly.

37
00:02:02,270 --> 00:02:04,550
Maybe we should start with the basics.

38
00:02:04,550 --> 00:02:06,830
What is the purpose of a self-introduction

39
00:02:06,830 --> 00:02:08,110
in a business meeting?

40
00:02:08,110 --> 00:02:09,350
Great idea.

41
00:02:09,350 --> 00:02:11,290
The main purpose is to give people

42
00:02:11,290 --> 00:02:13,790
a clear understanding of who you are,

43
00:02:13,790 --> 00:02:16,750
what you do, and why you're in that meeting.

44
00:02:16,750 --> 00:02:19,230
So it's not just about your name.

45
00:02:19,230 --> 00:02:20,230
Not at all.

46
00:02:20,230 --> 00:02:22,190
Your name is just the beginning.

47
00:02:22,190 --> 00:02:26,470
A strong introduction usually includes four key elements.

48
00:02:26,470 --> 00:02:27,870
Four elements, okay?

49
00:02:27,870 --> 00:02:29,110
Let's break that down.

50
00:02:29,110 --> 00:02:30,630
First, your name.

51
00:02:30,630 --> 00:02:31,830
Simple enough.

52
00:02:31,830 --> 00:02:34,630
Second, your job title or role.

53
00:02:34,630 --> 00:02:36,870
So people understand your position.

54
00:02:36,870 --> 00:02:37,950
Exactly.

55
00:02:37,950 --> 00:02:41,310
Third, the company or department you represent.

56
00:02:41,310 --> 00:02:42,590
And the fourth.

57
00:02:42,590 --> 00:02:44,190
The reason you're in the meeting

58
00:02:44,190 --> 00:02:46,590
or how you're connected to the project.

59
00:02:46,590 --> 00:02:47,710
That makes sense.

60
00:02:47,710 --> 00:02:50,910
So instead of just saying, hi, I'm Daniel Lee,

61
00:02:50,910 --> 00:02:53,110
I could say something more complete.

62
00:02:53,110 --> 00:02:56,990
Yes, for example, hi everyone, I'm Daniel Lee.

63
00:02:56,990 --> 00:02:59,630
I'm the operations manager at Global Tech

64
00:02:59,630 --> 00:03:03,310
and I oversee our regional supply chain projects.

65
00:03:03,310 --> 00:03:06,750
I'll be supporting the implementation phase of this project.

66
00:03:06,750 --> 00:03:10,510
That sounds clear, professional, and confident.

67
00:03:10,510 --> 00:03:11,390
Exactly.

68
00:03:11,390 --> 00:03:14,790
It tells people who you are and why you're relevant.

69
00:03:14,790 --> 00:03:16,270
Let's talk about tone.

70
00:03:16,270 --> 00:03:18,810
Should introductions always be formal?

71
00:03:18,810 --> 00:03:19,770
Good question.

72
00:03:19,770 --> 00:03:21,430
It depends on the meeting.

73
00:03:21,430 --> 00:03:24,410
In a very formal meeting with senior executives

74
00:03:24,410 --> 00:03:28,030
or clients, you should use a more professional tone.

75
00:03:28,030 --> 00:03:30,510
And in a small internal team meeting?

76
00:03:30,510 --> 00:03:34,150
You can be slightly more relaxed, but still professional.

77
00:03:34,150 --> 00:03:36,470
So let's compare two examples.

78
00:03:36,470 --> 00:03:37,310
OK.

79
00:03:37,310 --> 00:03:38,590
Formal version.

80
00:03:38,590 --> 00:03:39,750
Good morning, everyone.

81
00:03:39,750 --> 00:03:42,710
My name is Daniel Lee, and I'm the Senior Operations

82
00:03:42,710 --> 00:03:44,470
Manager at GlobalTech.

83
00:03:44,470 --> 00:03:47,430
I'm responsible for overseeing cross-border logistics

84
00:03:47,430 --> 00:03:49,310
and process optimization.

85
00:03:49,310 --> 00:03:51,470
I look forward to collaborating with you.

86
00:03:51,470 --> 00:03:52,310
Excellent.

87
00:03:52,310 --> 00:03:55,150
Now a slightly less formal version.

88
00:03:55,150 --> 00:03:56,070
Hi, everyone.

89
00:03:56,070 --> 00:03:56,990
I'm Daniel.

90
00:03:56,990 --> 00:03:59,070
I manage operations at GlobalTech,

91
00:03:59,070 --> 00:04:02,430
mainly focusing on logistics and process improvements.

92
00:04:02,430 --> 00:04:04,990
I'm excited to work with you on this project.

93
00:04:04,990 --> 00:04:06,470
Both are correct.

94
00:04:06,470 --> 00:04:09,430
The difference is in the level of formality.

95
00:04:09,430 --> 00:04:12,590
So learners need to consider the audience.

96
00:04:12,590 --> 00:04:13,790
Exactly.

97
00:04:13,790 --> 00:04:16,390
Always think, who am I speaking to?

98
00:04:16,390 --> 00:04:18,950
Let's talk about a very common situation,

99
00:04:18,950 --> 00:04:21,550
joining a meeting as a new employee.

100
00:04:21,550 --> 00:04:22,910
That's a big one.

101
00:04:22,910 --> 00:04:25,030
On your first day or first week,

102
00:04:25,030 --> 00:04:27,990
you'll probably be asked to introduce yourself.

103
00:04:27,990 --> 00:04:30,350
And sometimes you're not prepared.

104
00:04:30,350 --> 00:04:34,310
Yes, so here's a simple structure you can memorize.

105
00:04:34,310 --> 00:04:35,470
Great, let's hear it.

106
00:04:35,470 --> 00:04:38,290
Start with a greeting, then your name,

107
00:04:38,290 --> 00:04:42,110
then your previous experience, then your current role,

108
00:04:42,110 --> 00:04:45,670
and finally something positive about joining the team.

109
00:04:45,670 --> 00:04:47,350
Let's create an example.

110
00:04:47,350 --> 00:04:48,190
Sure.

111
00:04:48,190 --> 00:04:49,270
Good morning, everyone.

112
00:04:49,270 --> 00:04:50,750
My name is Daniel Lee,

113
00:04:50,750 --> 00:04:52,630
and I've just joined the marketing team

114
00:04:52,630 --> 00:04:55,150
as a business development executive.

115
00:04:55,150 --> 00:04:57,630
Previously, I worked at Bright Solutions,

116
00:04:57,630 --> 00:04:59,510
where I focused on client acquisition

117
00:04:59,510 --> 00:05:01,230
and strategic partnerships.

118
00:05:01,230 --> 00:05:03,310
I'm really excited to be part of this team

119
00:05:03,310 --> 00:05:05,470
and look forward to working with all of you.

120
00:05:05,470 --> 00:05:09,130
That's perfect, clear, structured and positive.

121
00:05:09,130 --> 00:05:12,510
Why is it important to mention previous experience?

122
00:05:12,510 --> 00:05:14,470
Because it builds credibility.

123
00:05:14,470 --> 00:05:16,670
It shows what you bring to the team.

124
00:05:16,670 --> 00:05:20,010
And credibility is very important in business.

125
00:05:20,010 --> 00:05:22,590
Absolutely, it helps people trust you.

126
00:05:22,590 --> 00:05:24,630
Let's talk about vocabulary.

127
00:05:24,630 --> 00:05:27,790
What are some useful verbs for introductions?

128
00:05:27,790 --> 00:05:28,750
Good idea.

129
00:05:28,750 --> 00:05:31,110
Instead of always saying I do,

130
00:05:31,110 --> 00:05:34,670
you can say I manage, I oversee, I lead,

131
00:05:34,670 --> 00:05:38,590
I coordinate, I support, or I specialize in.

132
00:05:38,590 --> 00:05:41,070
Those sound much more professional.

133
00:05:41,070 --> 00:05:41,910
Yes.

134
00:05:41,910 --> 00:05:44,590
For example, instead of saying I do sales,

135
00:05:44,590 --> 00:05:46,710
you can say I lead the sales team

136
00:05:46,710 --> 00:05:49,550
or I manage key client accounts.

137
00:05:49,550 --> 00:05:51,190
That sounds stronger.

138
00:05:51,190 --> 00:05:53,950
because it shows responsibility.

139
00:05:53,950 --> 00:05:56,310
Let's practice another situation,

140
00:05:56,310 --> 00:05:59,070
introducing yourself in a client meeting.

141
00:05:59,070 --> 00:06:01,710
In that case, you should be clear about your role

142
00:06:01,710 --> 00:06:03,470
in relation to the client.

143
00:06:03,470 --> 00:06:06,310
So maybe something like, good afternoon,

144
00:06:06,310 --> 00:06:09,750
I'm Daniel Lee, the account manager for your region.

145
00:06:09,750 --> 00:06:11,750
I'll be your main point of contact

146
00:06:11,750 --> 00:06:13,270
throughout this project.

147
00:06:13,270 --> 00:06:14,110
Perfect.

148
00:06:14,110 --> 00:06:17,590
The phrase main point of contact is very useful.

149
00:06:17,590 --> 00:06:19,670
It means you're the primary person

150
00:06:19,670 --> 00:06:21,350
they should communicate with.

151
00:06:21,350 --> 00:06:22,390
Exactly.

152
00:06:22,390 --> 00:06:24,190
What about virtual meetings?

153
00:06:24,190 --> 00:06:27,550
Are introductions different on Zoom or Teams?

154
00:06:27,550 --> 00:06:28,910
The structure is similar,

155
00:06:28,910 --> 00:06:32,870
but you may need to speak more clearly and slightly slower.

156
00:06:32,870 --> 00:06:34,870
Because of audio delays.

157
00:06:34,870 --> 00:06:38,270
Yes, and you might also mention where you're joining from.

158
00:06:38,270 --> 00:06:41,430
For example, hi everyone, I'm Daniel Lee,

159
00:06:41,430 --> 00:06:43,790
joining from our Singapore office.

160
00:06:43,790 --> 00:06:45,790
I manage regional operations

161
00:06:45,790 --> 00:06:49,110
and will be supporting the rollout in Southeast Asia.

162
00:06:49,110 --> 00:06:54,110
That's excellent. Mentioning location is especially helpful in international meetings.

163
00:06:54,110 --> 00:06:56,610
Let's talk about common mistakes.

164
00:06:56,610 --> 00:07:01,110
Good idea. One common mistake is giving too much personal information.

165
00:07:01,110 --> 00:07:03,610
Like talking about hobbies.

166
00:07:03,610 --> 00:07:08,610
Exactly. In a formal business meeting, you don't need to mention your favorite sports team.

167
00:07:08,610 --> 00:07:11,110
Unless it's a team-building session.

168
00:07:11,110 --> 00:07:14,110
Right. Another mistake is being too vague.

169
00:07:14,110 --> 00:07:15,610
For example?

170
00:07:15,610 --> 00:07:20,410
Saying something like, I work in a company without explaining your role.

171
00:07:20,410 --> 00:07:22,810
That doesn't tell people anything useful.

172
00:07:22,810 --> 00:07:23,810
Exactly.

173
00:07:23,810 --> 00:07:25,570
Be specific.

174
00:07:25,570 --> 00:07:28,410
Another mistake is apologizing too much.

175
00:07:28,410 --> 00:07:29,410
Yes.

176
00:07:29,410 --> 00:07:32,770
Starting with, sorry, I'm not very good at English.

177
00:07:32,770 --> 00:07:35,530
That reduces your confidence immediately.

178
00:07:35,530 --> 00:07:37,930
Instead, focus on your strengths.

179
00:07:37,930 --> 00:07:43,650
Let's create a longer example of a strong introduction in a senior-level meeting.

180
00:07:43,650 --> 00:07:44,650
Good idea.

181
00:07:44,650 --> 00:07:45,790
Good morning, everyone.

182
00:07:45,790 --> 00:07:50,410
My name is Daniel Lee, and I'm the Regional Operations Director at GlobalTech.

183
00:07:50,410 --> 00:07:55,050
I've been with the company for eight years, primarily focusing on process optimization

184
00:07:55,050 --> 00:07:57,610
and cross-border logistic strategy.

185
00:07:57,610 --> 00:08:01,850
In my current role, I oversee a team of 35 across three countries.

186
00:08:01,850 --> 00:08:06,810
Today, I'll be presenting our performance metrics and outlining our proposed expansion

187
00:08:06,810 --> 00:08:07,810
plan.

188
00:08:07,810 --> 00:08:11,430
That sounds very professional and leadership-oriented.

189
00:08:11,430 --> 00:08:15,950
Notice how it includes experience, responsibility, and purpose.

190
00:08:15,950 --> 00:08:16,950
Exactly.

191
00:08:16,950 --> 00:08:17,950
That's the key.

192
00:08:17,950 --> 00:08:20,990
Let's talk about body language briefly.

193
00:08:20,990 --> 00:08:22,270
Very important.

194
00:08:22,270 --> 00:08:26,150
Even in English learning, nonverbal communication matters.

195
00:08:26,150 --> 00:08:28,030
So what should you do?

196
00:08:28,030 --> 00:08:34,230
Maintain eye contact, smile naturally, sit or stand upright, and speak clearly.

197
00:08:34,230 --> 00:08:36,590
And avoid speaking too fast.

198
00:08:36,590 --> 00:08:41,270
Yes, speaking slowly shows confidence, not weakness.

199
00:08:41,270 --> 00:08:42,650
That's a great point.

200
00:08:42,650 --> 00:08:46,770
Now let's review a simple formula our listeners can remember.

201
00:08:46,770 --> 00:08:47,770
I like formulas.

202
00:08:47,770 --> 00:08:49,270
They're easy to follow.

203
00:08:49,270 --> 00:08:50,270
Here it is.

204
00:08:50,270 --> 00:08:58,350
Greeting plus name plus position plus company plus key responsibility plus purpose.

205
00:08:58,350 --> 00:09:00,710
Let's say that again slowly.

206
00:09:00,710 --> 00:09:07,110
Greeting name position company responsibility purpose.

207
00:09:07,110 --> 00:09:11,590
If you follow that structure, your introduction will always be clear.

208
00:09:11,590 --> 00:09:15,270
Before we finish, let's practice one more realistic scenario.

209
00:09:15,270 --> 00:09:16,270
Okay.

210
00:09:16,270 --> 00:09:21,830
You're in a cross-functional project meeting with people from finance, marketing and IT.

211
00:09:21,830 --> 00:09:28,310
So I might say, hi everyone, I'm Erica Chen, the project manager from the product development

212
00:09:28,310 --> 00:09:29,310
team.

213
00:09:29,310 --> 00:09:33,950
I coordinate cross-department collaboration for our new product launches.

214
00:09:33,950 --> 00:09:39,870
I'll be overseeing timelines and ensuring we stay aligned with our strategic objectives.

215
00:09:39,870 --> 00:09:42,550
That sounds confident and organized.

216
00:09:42,550 --> 00:09:43,550
Thank you.

217
00:09:43,550 --> 00:09:46,790
Now let's quickly review some key vocabulary from today.

218
00:09:46,790 --> 00:09:48,990
First word, introduce.

219
00:09:48,990 --> 00:09:51,630
To present yourself to others for the first time.

220
00:09:51,630 --> 00:09:53,750
Second word, roll.

221
00:09:53,750 --> 00:09:56,350
Your function or position in a company.

222
00:09:56,350 --> 00:09:58,510
Third word, oversee.

223
00:09:58,510 --> 00:10:00,670
To supervise or manage.

224
00:10:00,670 --> 00:10:02,950
Fourth word, collaborate.

225
00:10:02,950 --> 00:10:04,950
to work together with others.

226
00:10:04,950 --> 00:10:07,350
Fifth word, responsibility.

227
00:10:07,350 --> 00:10:10,430
A duty or task you are accountable for.

228
00:10:10,430 --> 00:10:12,670
Sixth word, credibility.

229
00:10:12,670 --> 00:10:15,450
The quality of being trusted and believed.

230
00:10:15,450 --> 00:10:17,750
And finally, professional.

231
00:10:17,750 --> 00:10:19,670
Showing competence, respect,

232
00:10:19,670 --> 00:10:21,990
and appropriate behavior in business.

233
00:10:21,990 --> 00:10:24,390
And finally, confidence.

234
00:10:24,390 --> 00:10:26,710
Well, Daniel, we covered a lot today.

235
00:10:26,710 --> 00:10:28,030
We really did.

236
00:10:28,030 --> 00:10:30,510
Introducing yourself may seem simple,

237
00:10:30,510 --> 00:10:34,310
but doing it well can make a huge difference in your career.

238
00:10:34,310 --> 00:10:36,710
Remember, preparation is key.

239
00:10:36,710 --> 00:10:39,510
Practice your introduction before the meeting.

240
00:10:39,510 --> 00:10:41,950
Keep it clear and structured.

241
00:10:41,950 --> 00:10:44,470
Adapt your tone to the situation.

242
00:10:44,470 --> 00:10:47,830
And most importantly, speak with confidence.

243
00:10:47,830 --> 00:10:50,590
Now it's time for our question of the day.

244
00:10:50,590 --> 00:10:52,430
Yes, we love hearing from you.

245
00:10:52,430 --> 00:10:55,870
How do you usually introduce yourself in a business meeting?

246
00:10:55,870 --> 00:10:58,830
Do you feel confident or do you feel nervous?

247
00:10:58,830 --> 00:11:03,830
Try writing your self-introduction in English and practice saying it out loud.

248
00:11:03,830 --> 00:11:07,270
The more you practice, the more natural it will feel.

249
00:11:07,270 --> 00:11:09,350
Thank you so much for spending your time.

250
00:11:09,350 --> 00:11:13,230
Hello and welcome to Business English Conversations.

251
00:11:13,230 --> 00:11:14,230
Hi everyone.

252
00:11:14,230 --> 00:11:16,270
We're so glad you're joining us today.

253
00:11:16,270 --> 00:11:20,870
On this podcast, we help you improve your professional English through natural, practical

254
00:11:20,870 --> 00:11:22,950
workplace conversations.

255
00:11:22,950 --> 00:11:26,790
And today, we're going to focus on a skill that can completely change the way people

256
00:11:26,790 --> 00:11:28,390
see you at work.

257
00:11:28,390 --> 00:11:33,350
That's right! Today's topic is how to introduce yourself in a business meeting.

258
00:11:33,350 --> 00:11:36,350
It sounds simple, but it can feel very stressful.

259
00:11:36,350 --> 00:11:41,310
Especially if it's your first day, your first international meeting, or your first time speaking

260
00:11:41,310 --> 00:11:42,950
in English at work.

261
00:11:42,950 --> 00:11:47,750
Exactly! A self-introduction is often your first opportunity to create a professional

262
00:11:47,750 --> 00:11:48,910
impression.

263
00:11:48,910 --> 00:11:51,470
And first impressions are powerful.

264
00:11:51,470 --> 00:11:56,550
So let's start with a basic question. Why is introducing yourself properly so important

265
00:11:56,550 --> 00:11:57,950
in business?

266
00:11:57,950 --> 00:12:03,290
Because it helps people understand who you are, what you do, and why you are in the meeting.

267
00:12:03,290 --> 00:12:06,490
And it establishes credibility from the beginning.

268
00:12:06,490 --> 00:12:10,330
Credibility means people trust your expertise and take you seriously.

269
00:12:10,330 --> 00:12:14,890
When you introduce yourself clearly and confidently, people are more likely to respect your ideas

270
00:12:14,890 --> 00:12:16,390
later in the meeting.

271
00:12:16,390 --> 00:12:17,650
That's such a good point.

272
00:12:17,650 --> 00:12:21,210
Your introduction sets the tone for everything that follows.

273
00:12:21,210 --> 00:12:22,690
So let's break it down.

274
00:12:22,690 --> 00:12:25,970
What should a professional introduction include?

275
00:12:25,970 --> 00:12:32,250
five key elements, a greeting, your name, your role, your department or company, and

276
00:12:32,250 --> 00:12:35,250
your main responsibility related to the meeting.

277
00:12:35,250 --> 00:12:38,090
And sometimes a positive closing sentence.

278
00:12:38,090 --> 00:12:42,290
Yes, something that shows enthusiasm or collaboration.

279
00:12:42,290 --> 00:12:45,370
Let's imagine it's your first team meeting at a new company.

280
00:12:45,370 --> 00:12:47,290
How might you introduce yourself?

281
00:12:47,290 --> 00:12:48,890
It could sound like this.

282
00:12:48,890 --> 00:12:49,970
Good morning, everyone.

283
00:12:49,970 --> 00:12:54,770
My name is James Walker, and I've just joined the operations team as a supply chain

284
00:12:54,770 --> 00:12:55,770
analyst.

285
00:12:55,770 --> 00:12:58,170
I'll be focusing on process optimization

286
00:12:58,170 --> 00:12:59,890
and vendor coordination.

287
00:12:59,890 --> 00:13:02,530
I'm really looking forward to working with all of you.

288
00:13:02,530 --> 00:13:04,890
That sounds structured and professional.

289
00:13:04,890 --> 00:13:06,970
Thank you, let's analyze it.

290
00:13:06,970 --> 00:13:08,730
You started with a greeting.

291
00:13:08,730 --> 00:13:10,250
Always start politely.

292
00:13:10,250 --> 00:13:13,370
Good morning, good afternoon, or hello, everyone.

293
00:13:13,370 --> 00:13:15,210
Then you gave your full name.

294
00:13:15,210 --> 00:13:17,450
Yes, especially informal settings

295
00:13:17,450 --> 00:13:19,570
or when people don't know you yet.

296
00:13:19,570 --> 00:13:22,330
After that, you mentioned your role in department.

297
00:13:22,330 --> 00:13:23,890
That provides context.

298
00:13:23,890 --> 00:13:27,610
people need to understand where you fit in the organization.

299
00:13:27,610 --> 00:13:30,570
And you briefly explained your responsibilities.

300
00:13:30,570 --> 00:13:33,810
That helps colleagues see how they might collaborate with me.

301
00:13:33,810 --> 00:13:36,130
And you ended with a positive statement.

302
00:13:36,130 --> 00:13:37,130
Exactly.

303
00:13:37,130 --> 00:13:40,450
Ending with enthusiasm creates a friendly tone.

304
00:13:40,450 --> 00:13:42,690
Now let's imagine a different scenario.

305
00:13:42,690 --> 00:13:45,050
You're meeting a client for the first time.

306
00:13:45,050 --> 00:13:48,610
In that case, clarity becomes even more important.

307
00:13:48,610 --> 00:13:49,690
Why?

308
00:13:49,690 --> 00:13:53,850
Because clients need to know exactly who is responsible for what.

309
00:13:53,850 --> 00:13:55,290
Can you give an example?

310
00:13:55,290 --> 00:13:56,290
Sure.

311
00:13:56,290 --> 00:13:59,230
Good afternoon and thank you for joining today's meeting.

312
00:13:59,230 --> 00:14:04,130
My name is Olivia Chen and I'm the Account Manager at Strategic Growth Partners.

313
00:14:04,130 --> 00:14:09,530
I'll be overseeing your project and ensuring that all deliverables are completed on schedule.

314
00:14:09,530 --> 00:14:13,290
Please feel free to contact me directly if you have any questions.

315
00:14:13,290 --> 00:14:15,090
That's very reassuring.

316
00:14:15,090 --> 00:14:19,090
Yes, it communicates responsibility and accessibility.

317
00:14:19,090 --> 00:14:21,930
I noticed you said overseeing your project.

318
00:14:21,930 --> 00:14:23,610
What does overseeing mean?

319
00:14:23,610 --> 00:14:28,610
It means supervising or managing something to make sure it runs smoothly.

320
00:14:28,610 --> 00:14:30,510
Let's talk about tone.

321
00:14:30,510 --> 00:14:32,550
Tone is extremely important.

322
00:14:32,550 --> 00:14:36,230
Your voice should be steady, clear, and confident.

323
00:14:36,230 --> 00:14:39,070
Not too quickly and not too quiet.

324
00:14:39,070 --> 00:14:40,070
Exactly.

325
00:14:40,070 --> 00:14:43,150
Speaking too quickly can make you sound nervous.

326
00:14:43,150 --> 00:14:45,950
Many people feel nervous during introductions.

327
00:14:45,950 --> 00:14:47,630
That's completely normal.

328
00:14:47,630 --> 00:14:51,070
Even experienced professionals feel that way sometimes.

329
00:14:51,070 --> 00:14:53,670
So, what can help reduce that nervousness?

330
00:14:53,670 --> 00:14:55,870
Preparation and practice.

331
00:14:55,870 --> 00:14:58,670
Should people write down their introduction beforehand?

332
00:14:58,670 --> 00:15:02,630
Yes, especially if English is not their first language.

333
00:15:02,630 --> 00:15:05,390
Writing it down helps you organize your thoughts.

334
00:15:05,390 --> 00:15:08,150
But maybe don't read directly from the paper.

335
00:15:08,150 --> 00:15:09,150
Right.

336
00:15:09,150 --> 00:15:11,710
It's better to practice until you can say it naturally.

337
00:15:11,710 --> 00:15:14,630
Let's move to virtual needings.

338
00:15:14,630 --> 00:15:17,990
Virtual needings require slightly different strategies.

339
00:15:17,990 --> 00:15:19,670
Because of technical issues?

340
00:15:19,670 --> 00:15:20,670
Exactly.

341
00:15:20,670 --> 00:15:26,270
be audio delays or background noise. What would a strong virtual introduction sound like?

342
00:15:26,270 --> 00:15:32,030
Something like this. Hi everyone, I hope you can hear me clearly. My name is Ricardo Silva,

343
00:15:32,030 --> 00:15:36,910
and I'm the Regional Sales Manager for Latin America. I'll be presenting our quarterly

344
00:15:36,910 --> 00:15:42,830
performance figures and discussing our expansion strategy. That's clear and focused. Yes,

345
00:15:42,830 --> 00:15:48,190
and in virtual meetings, clarity is essential. Let's talk about large meetings where many

346
00:15:48,190 --> 00:15:50,790
Many people are introducing themselves quickly.

347
00:15:50,790 --> 00:15:54,070
In that situation, brevity is key.

348
00:15:54,070 --> 00:15:56,510
Brevity means being short and concise.

349
00:15:56,510 --> 00:15:57,590
Exactly.

350
00:15:57,590 --> 00:15:59,070
Can you give an example?

351
00:15:59,070 --> 00:16:02,150
Hello, I'm Nina Patel from the Compliance Team.

352
00:16:02,150 --> 00:16:06,350
I handle regulatory reporting and internal audits.

353
00:16:06,350 --> 00:16:07,350
Very efficient.

354
00:16:07,350 --> 00:16:10,030
Yes, it respects everyone's time.

355
00:16:10,030 --> 00:16:13,990
Now let's discuss introducing yourself as a senior leader.

356
00:16:13,990 --> 00:16:17,350
Senior leaders often highlight strategy and vision.

357
00:16:17,350 --> 00:16:22,950
their role affects the whole organization. Exactly. What might that sound like?

358
00:16:22,950 --> 00:16:29,650
Good morning. I'm Jonathan Reed, Chief Technology Officer. My focus is on driving digital transformation

359
00:16:29,650 --> 00:16:34,710
across the company and ensuring we remain competitive in the market. I look forward to

360
00:16:34,710 --> 00:16:39,190
working closely with each department to achieve our long-term objectives.

361
00:16:39,190 --> 00:16:44,550
That sounds visionary and authoritative. And that's appropriate for leadership roles.

362
00:16:44,550 --> 00:16:47,410
Let's talk about cross-functional meetings.

363
00:16:47,410 --> 00:16:50,430
Those meetings include people from different departments.

364
00:16:50,430 --> 00:16:52,910
So you need to clarify your perspective.

365
00:16:52,910 --> 00:16:54,070
Exactly.

366
00:16:54,070 --> 00:16:55,230
Can you demonstrate?

367
00:16:55,230 --> 00:16:56,230
Hello everyone.

368
00:16:56,230 --> 00:16:59,230
I'm Maria Gomez from the Product Development Team.

369
00:16:59,230 --> 00:17:02,790
I focus on user experience and feature innovation.

370
00:17:02,790 --> 00:17:07,910
I'm here to ensure that customer feedback is reflected in our new product design.

371
00:17:07,910 --> 00:17:10,150
That clearly explains your contribution.

372
00:17:10,150 --> 00:17:14,150
Yes, it shows how my role connects to the meeting goals.

373
00:17:14,150 --> 00:17:17,390
Now let's discuss common mistakes people make.

374
00:17:17,390 --> 00:17:20,930
One common mistake is apologizing unnecessarily.

375
00:17:20,930 --> 00:17:24,070
Like saying, sorry, my English isn't very good.

376
00:17:24,070 --> 00:17:25,070
Exactly.

377
00:17:25,070 --> 00:17:27,730
That immediately reduces confidence.

378
00:17:27,730 --> 00:17:29,090
Another mistake?

379
00:17:29,090 --> 00:17:30,790
Speaking too vaguely.

380
00:17:30,790 --> 00:17:31,790
For example?

381
00:17:31,790 --> 00:17:35,430
I work in the company, doesn't provide enough detail.

382
00:17:35,430 --> 00:17:37,630
Or speaking for too long.

383
00:17:37,630 --> 00:17:40,530
Yes, especially when time is limited.

384
00:17:40,530 --> 00:17:42,430
What about body language?

385
00:17:42,430 --> 00:17:48,150
language communicates confidence. Stand or sit upright, maintain eye contact, and

386
00:17:48,150 --> 00:17:53,750
smile naturally. Even in virtual meetings, looking at the camera helps. Yes, it creates

387
00:17:53,750 --> 00:17:58,030
a sense of connection. Now let's imagine you've been promoted internally and

388
00:17:58,030 --> 00:18:02,510
you're leading a meeting for the first time. That's a special situation. Because

389
00:18:02,510 --> 00:18:07,230
people already know you, but your role has changed. Exactly. What could you

390
00:18:07,230 --> 00:18:11,830
say? Good morning everyone. As many of you know, I've recently taken on the

391
00:18:11,830 --> 00:18:16,710
role of team leader. For those I haven't worked closely with, I'm Daniel Foster.

392
00:18:16,710 --> 00:18:20,870
I've been with the company for six years in the sales department and I'm excited to

393
00:18:20,870 --> 00:18:26,630
continue building our success together. That acknowledges both familiarity and change.

394
00:18:26,630 --> 00:18:30,630
Yes, it reinforces experience while signaling readership.

395
00:18:30,630 --> 00:18:35,830
Let's also talk about cultural awareness. Very important in global business.

396
00:18:35,830 --> 00:18:39,190
Some cultures prefer very formal introductions.

397
00:18:39,190 --> 00:18:42,630
Others are more informal and use first names immediately.

398
00:18:42,630 --> 00:18:45,670
If you're unsure, what's the safest approach?

399
00:18:45,670 --> 00:18:48,790
Start formal. You can always adjust later.

400
00:18:48,790 --> 00:18:50,230
That's smart advice.

401
00:18:50,230 --> 00:18:53,510
Professionalism should always be your starting point.

402
00:18:53,510 --> 00:18:59,830
Before we close, let's do one extended, polished introduction that combines everything we've discussed.

403
00:18:59,830 --> 00:19:01,030
Great idea.

404
00:19:01,030 --> 00:19:04,150
Good afternoon, everyone, and thank you for being here today.

405
00:19:04,150 --> 00:19:10,630
My name is Alexandra Novak and I've recently joined the organization as Director of Corporate Strategy.

406
00:19:10,630 --> 00:19:16,150
In my role, I'll be working closely with senior leadership to define our long-term objectives

407
00:19:16,150 --> 00:19:19,670
and identify growth opportunities across international markets.

408
00:19:20,230 --> 00:19:24,550
I'm particularly interested in fostering collaboration between departments

409
00:19:24,550 --> 00:19:28,310
to ensure our strategy is both innovative and practical.

410
00:19:28,310 --> 00:19:33,590
I'm truly looking forward to working with all of you and contributing to our continued success.

411
00:19:33,590 --> 00:19:37,030
That was excellent, confident, clear, and purposeful.

412
00:19:37,030 --> 00:19:41,190
And that's exactly what a strong business introduction should achieve.

413
00:19:41,190 --> 00:19:44,790
Today we explored how to introduce yourself in internal meetings,

414
00:19:44,790 --> 00:19:49,670
client meetings, virtual calls, large group sessions, and leadership settings.

415
00:19:49,670 --> 00:19:55,670
We talked about structure, tone, body language, cultural awareness, and common mistakes.

416
00:19:55,670 --> 00:19:59,030
Remember, a great introduction doesn't have to be long.

417
00:19:59,030 --> 00:20:02,150
It just needs to be clear, confident, and relevant.

418
00:20:02,150 --> 00:20:04,150
Now it's your turn.

419
00:20:04,150 --> 00:20:08,150
Practice creating your own professional introduction using the structure we discussed.

420
00:20:08,150 --> 00:20:11,150
Say it out loud. Record yourself if possible.

421
00:20:11,150 --> 00:20:14,150
The more you practice, the more natural it will feel.

422
00:20:14,150 --> 00:20:18,150
Thank you for listening to Business English Conversations.

423
00:20:18,150 --> 00:20:22,150
We appreciate your commitment to improving your professional communication skills.

424
00:20:22,150 --> 00:20:25,150
Keep practicing and keep building your confidence.

