00:26:30,270 --> 00:26:37,070
um or uh, and keep your voice steady. What are filler words? Words or sounds we use when we're

516
00:26:37,070 --> 00:26:43,470
nervous or thinking. For example, um, like, or you know. Those can reduce professionalism.

517
00:26:43,470 --> 00:26:48,270
Yes, especially in formal business meetings. Now let's talk about body language in

518
00:26:48,270 --> 00:26:52,350
face-to-face meetings. Body language is extremely important.

519
00:26:52,350 --> 00:26:57,390
Maintain eye contact, sit upright, and offer a firm handshake if appropriate.

520
00:26:57,390 --> 00:27:03,150
and smile naturally. Yes, a natural smile shows confidence and approachability.

521
00:27:03,150 --> 00:27:08,430
Now I want to discuss a challenging situation. What if you forget what you plan to say?

522
00:27:08,430 --> 00:27:14,990
That happens. If it does, keep it simple. Say your name, your role, and one key responsibility.

523
00:27:14,990 --> 00:27:21,230
That's enough. So something like, Hi, I'm Speaker 2. I work in finance, focusing on

524
00:27:21,230 --> 00:27:25,790
budgeting and forecasting. Exactly. Simple is better than complicated.

525
00:27:25,790 --> 00:27:30,910
What about when you're leading the meeting? Then your introduction includes leadership context.

526
00:27:30,910 --> 00:27:36,670
For example, good morning everyone. I'm Speaker 2, Director of Marketing. I'll be facilitating

527
00:27:36,670 --> 00:27:42,030
today's discussion on our quarterly strategy. Thank you all for joining. Facilitating means

528
00:27:42,030 --> 00:27:47,390
guiding or leading the meeting. Correct. Now let's talk about networking events. That's

529
00:27:47,390 --> 00:27:52,110
slightly different from formal meetings. Yes, networking introductions are usually

530
00:27:52,110 --> 00:27:58,190
shorter and more conversational. Like an elevator pitch? Exactly. An elevator pitch is a brief and

531
00:27:58,190 --> 00:28:05,150
persuasive self-introduction. Can you give an example? Sure. Hi, I'm Speaker 2. I work in digital

532
00:28:05,150 --> 00:28:10,750
marketing, helping brands improve their online visibility and customer engagement. What about you?

533
00:28:10,750 --> 00:28:15,630
I like that you ended with a question. Yes, because networking is about conversation,

534
00:28:15,630 --> 00:28:20,910
not just talking about yourself. Let's review some useful phrases our listeners can practice.

535
00:28:20,910 --> 00:28:30,270
Good idea. Phrases like I'm responsible for, I oversee, I specialize in, I focus on, I'm currently

536
00:28:30,270 --> 00:28:37,150
working on. Those phrases make your introduction more dynamic. Yes, they show action and responsibility.

537
00:28:37,150 --> 00:28:43,310
Now let's talk about common mistakes. What should people avoid? Avoid speaking too quietly.

538
00:28:43,310 --> 00:28:49,150
Avoid giving too much personal information. Avoid negative statements like I'm new so I

539
00:28:49,150 --> 00:28:55,310
I don't know much. That sounds insecure. Exactly. Instead of saying I don't know much, say I'm

540
00:28:55,310 --> 00:29:01,390
looking forward to learning more. That's much more positive. Also, avoid using overly casual

541
00:29:01,390 --> 00:29:07,710
language in formal meetings. For example, instead of saying hey guys, say good morning everyone.

542
00:29:07,710 --> 00:29:14,030
That's a big difference. It is. Word choice shapes perception. Let's do a quick roleplay.

543
00:29:14,030 --> 00:29:16,670
I'll be a new employee introducing myself.

544
00:29:16,670 --> 00:29:17,950
Great, go ahead.

545
00:29:17,950 --> 00:29:20,030
Good morning. My name is Speaker 1,

546
00:29:20,030 --> 00:29:23,790
and I've recently joined the IT department as a systems engineer.

547
00:29:23,790 --> 00:29:25,870
I'll be managing network infrastructure

548
00:29:25,870 --> 00:29:28,190
and supporting cybersecurity initiatives.

549
00:29:28,190 --> 00:29:30,270
I'm excited to collaborate with all of you.

550
00:29:30,270 --> 00:29:33,390
Excellent, clear, confidence, and professional.

551
00:29:33,390 --> 00:29:34,190
Thank you.

552
00:29:34,190 --> 00:29:35,150
Now your turn.

553
00:29:35,150 --> 00:29:36,830
Pretend you're meeting a client.

554
00:29:36,830 --> 00:29:37,950
Good afternoon.

555
00:29:37,950 --> 00:29:39,390
My name is Speaker 2,

556
00:29:39,390 --> 00:29:43,070
and I'm the lead project manager overseeing this implementation.

557
00:29:43,070 --> 00:29:46,270
I'll be your primary point of contact throughout the project.

558
00:29:46,270 --> 00:29:50,030
We're looking forward to delivering strong results for your organization.

559
00:29:50,030 --> 00:29:51,790
That sounds very reassuring.

560
00:29:51,790 --> 00:29:53,790
That's important in client meetings.

561
00:29:53,790 --> 00:29:56,190
Clients want confidence and clarity.

562
00:29:56,190 --> 00:29:59,470
Let's review key vocabulary from today's conversation.

563
00:29:59,470 --> 00:30:00,350
Good idea.

564
00:30:00,350 --> 00:30:02,430
The first word is credibility.

565
00:30:02,430 --> 00:30:06,990
Credibility means being trusted and believed in in a professional context.

566
00:30:06,990 --> 00:30:08,670
Next is concise.

567
00:30:08,670 --> 00:30:12,990
Concise means short but containing all necessary information.

568
00:30:12,990 --> 00:30:18,430
Then we have facilitate. To facilitate means to guide or make a process easier.

569
00:30:18,430 --> 00:30:23,630
Another important phrase is point of contact. That means the main person responsible for

570
00:30:23,630 --> 00:30:29,710
communication. We also mentioned elevator pitch, which is a brief and persuasive self-introduction

571
00:30:29,710 --> 00:30:34,750
and responsibility, which refers to the duties or tasks you manage. All of these

572
00:30:34,750 --> 00:30:39,630
words are extremely useful when introducing yourself in business meetings. Before we finish,

573
00:30:39,630 --> 00:30:40,950
Let's summarize.

574
00:30:40,950 --> 00:30:46,930
A strong business introduction includes greeting, name, role, responsibility, and a positive

575
00:30:46,930 --> 00:30:48,370
closing statement.

576
00:30:48,370 --> 00:30:51,470
Keep it clear, confident, and concise.

577
00:30:51,470 --> 00:30:53,850
Adjust your tone depending on the situation.

578
00:30:53,850 --> 00:30:58,590
Internal meeting, client meeting, leadership role, or networking event.

579
00:30:58,590 --> 00:31:01,230
Practice in advance to reduce nervousness.

580
00:31:01,230 --> 00:31:04,230
And remember that confidence grows with repetition.

581
00:31:04,230 --> 00:31:06,390
Now it's time for our question of the day.

582
00:31:06,390 --> 00:31:10,230
Yes, how do you usually introduce yourself in professional meetings?

583
00:31:10,230 --> 00:31:13,930
Write a short business introduction about yourself and practice it out loud.

584
00:31:13,930 --> 00:31:19,430
You can say something like, good morning, my name is, I work as, and I'm responsible

585
00:31:19,430 --> 00:31:20,430
for.

586
00:31:20,430 --> 00:31:21,910
Practice until it feels natural.

587
00:31:21,910 --> 00:31:24,670
Remember, strong communication skills.

588
00:31:24,670 --> 00:31:32,720
Hello, and welcome to Business English Daily Conversation.

589
00:31:32,720 --> 00:31:34,080
Hi, everyone.

590
00:31:34,080 --> 00:31:37,620
We're really glad you're here with us today and ready to practice practical

591
00:31:37,620 --> 00:31:39,360
workplace English.

592
00:31:39,360 --> 00:31:44,240
On this channel, we focus on real professional communication, the kind you actually use in

593
00:31:44,240 --> 00:31:47,000
meetings, interviews, and client calls.

594
00:31:47,000 --> 00:31:51,040
If you enjoy learning with us, make sure you follow and share this with someone who wants

595
00:31:51,040 --> 00:31:53,680
to improve their business English skills.

596
00:31:53,680 --> 00:31:58,640
Today, we're going to talk about a skill that sounds simple but is often done poorly.

597
00:31:58,640 --> 00:32:01,200
How to introduce yourself in a business meeting.

598
00:32:01,200 --> 00:32:05,960
Yes, and this is something that happens in small meetings, large meetings, online

599
00:32:05,960 --> 00:32:09,840
meetings, client meetings, and even internal team calls.

600
00:32:09,840 --> 00:32:14,360
A strong introduction helps you sound confident, credible, and clear.

601
00:32:14,360 --> 00:32:18,000
A weak introduction makes you sound unsure or unprepared.

602
00:32:18,000 --> 00:32:23,000
I've seen very experienced professionals give bad introductions just because they never

603
00:32:23,000 --> 00:32:25,080
learned a simple structure.

604
00:32:25,080 --> 00:32:26,160
Exactly.

605
00:32:26,160 --> 00:32:30,520
So today we're going to walk through this as a natural conversation with examples,

606
00:32:30,520 --> 00:32:33,640
corrections, better versions, and useful phrases.

607
00:32:33,640 --> 00:32:35,640
Let's start with a basic situation.

608
00:32:35,640 --> 00:32:38,340
Imagine you join a meeting and the manager says,

609
00:32:38,340 --> 00:32:40,540
let's quickly introduce ourselves.

610
00:32:40,540 --> 00:32:42,220
What should you say first?

611
00:32:42,220 --> 00:32:45,340
First, always start with a greeting and your name.

612
00:32:45,340 --> 00:32:47,460
For example, good morning everyone.

613
00:32:47,460 --> 00:32:50,160
My name is Daniel Lee, simple and clear.

614
00:32:50,160 --> 00:32:52,620
Some learners say only I'm Daniel

615
00:32:52,620 --> 00:32:54,980
in a formal meeting, is that okay?

616
00:32:54,980 --> 00:32:57,500
It's not wrong, but it can sound too casual

617
00:32:57,500 --> 00:32:58,820
depending on the setting.

618
00:32:58,820 --> 00:33:00,860
In cross department or client meetings,

619
00:33:00,860 --> 00:33:02,340
use your full name.

620
00:33:02,340 --> 00:33:04,660
After your name, what comes next?

621
00:33:04,660 --> 00:33:06,360
your role or job title.

622
00:33:06,360 --> 00:33:09,320
For example, I'm a senior procurement analyst.

623
00:33:09,320 --> 00:33:11,600
That tells people your level and function.

624
00:33:11,600 --> 00:33:13,320
And after job title?

625
00:33:13,320 --> 00:33:16,600
Your responsibility or involvement in the meeting or project.

626
00:33:16,600 --> 00:33:18,880
That's the part many people forget.

627
00:33:18,880 --> 00:33:21,560
So a complete version sounds like what?

628
00:33:21,560 --> 00:33:22,400
Good morning.

629
00:33:22,400 --> 00:33:23,680
My name is Daniel Lee.

630
00:33:23,680 --> 00:33:25,320
I'm a senior procurement analyst,

631
00:33:25,320 --> 00:33:28,720
and I handle vendor selection and contract negotiations.

632
00:33:28,720 --> 00:33:31,200
That sounds professional and specific.

633
00:33:31,200 --> 00:33:34,240
It also helps others know when to talk to you later.

634
00:33:34,240 --> 00:33:39,040
Exactly. Good introductions reduce confusion and increase efficiency.

635
00:33:39,040 --> 00:33:42,880
Let's compare a weak and strong version. I'll give a weak one.

636
00:33:42,880 --> 00:33:45,760
Hi, I'm Daniel. I work in purchasing.

637
00:33:45,760 --> 00:33:50,080
Stronger version? Good morning. I'm Daniel Lee from the Procurement Team.

638
00:33:50,080 --> 00:33:53,520
I manage supplier evaluation and purchasing contracts.

639
00:33:53,520 --> 00:33:56,720
The second one sounds more confident and structured.

640
00:33:56,720 --> 00:34:00,640
Structure creates confidence, even if your English is not perfect.

641
00:34:00,640 --> 00:34:02,280
What about nervous speakers?

642
00:34:02,280 --> 00:34:05,000
Many people panic during introductions.

643
00:34:05,000 --> 00:34:07,200
Then use a memorized formula.

644
00:34:07,200 --> 00:34:10,840
Name, role, function, meeting connection.

645
00:34:10,840 --> 00:34:12,760
Repeat it until automatic.

646
00:34:12,760 --> 00:34:14,600
Let's practice variations.

647
00:34:14,600 --> 00:34:18,000
Suppose it's an internal team meeting with new members.

648
00:34:18,000 --> 00:34:20,200
Then you can be slightly warmer.

649
00:34:20,200 --> 00:34:21,160
Hello everyone.

650
00:34:21,160 --> 00:34:22,880
My name is Maria Gomez.

651
00:34:22,880 --> 00:34:24,680
I recently joined the finance team

652
00:34:24,680 --> 00:34:26,040
as a reporting specialist

653
00:34:26,040 --> 00:34:29,240
and I'll be supporting the monthly performance analysis.

654
00:34:29,240 --> 00:34:33,040
That phrase I recently joined is useful for newcomers.

655
00:34:33,040 --> 00:34:36,000
Yes, it gives context and invites patients.

656
00:34:36,000 --> 00:34:37,640
What if you're very senior?

657
00:34:37,640 --> 00:34:39,520
Should your introduction change?

658
00:34:39,520 --> 00:34:43,400
Yes, senior professionals should signal decision authority.

659
00:34:43,400 --> 00:34:45,320
Example, good afternoon.

660
00:34:45,320 --> 00:34:48,200
I'm Robert Chen, Director of Operations.

661
00:34:48,200 --> 00:34:51,440
I oversee regional delivery and process strategy.

662
00:34:51,440 --> 00:34:53,400
That signals scope and leadership

663
00:34:53,400 --> 00:34:55,240
without sounding arrogant.

664
00:34:55,240 --> 00:34:57,960
Correct, it's factual, not boastful.

665
00:34:57,960 --> 00:35:00,420
Now let's talk about virtual meetings.

666
00:35:00,420 --> 00:35:03,380
Online introductions are slightly different, right?

667
00:35:03,380 --> 00:35:04,300
Yes.

668
00:35:04,300 --> 00:35:06,580
In virtual meetings, clarity matters more

669
00:35:06,580 --> 00:35:09,500
because attention is lower and audio may cut out.

670
00:35:09,500 --> 00:35:11,420
So what should people add?

671
00:35:11,420 --> 00:35:13,900
Add team or location context.

672
00:35:13,900 --> 00:35:15,620
Hello, I'm Aisha Khan,

673
00:35:15,620 --> 00:35:17,780
product designer based in the London office,

674
00:35:17,780 --> 00:35:20,180
focusing on user interface systems.

675
00:35:20,180 --> 00:35:22,540
Location helps global teams.

676
00:35:22,540 --> 00:35:25,940
Exactly, especially in multinational companies.

677
00:35:25,940 --> 00:35:29,380
I've heard some people talk too long in introductions.

678
00:35:29,380 --> 00:35:30,940
That's a common mistake.

679
00:35:30,940 --> 00:35:33,220
Your introduction is not your biography.

680
00:35:33,220 --> 00:35:36,340
Keep it under 20 seconds unless asked for more detail.

681
00:35:36,340 --> 00:35:38,900
Let me try an overly long one.

682
00:35:38,900 --> 00:35:40,340
Hello, I'm David.

683
00:35:40,340 --> 00:35:41,980
I started five years ago.

684
00:35:41,980 --> 00:35:43,700
I worked in many areas.

685
00:35:43,700 --> 00:35:45,060
I like problem solving.

686
00:35:45,060 --> 00:35:47,500
I'm very passionate about teamwork.

687
00:35:47,500 --> 00:35:50,380
Yes, too personal and unfocused.

688
00:35:50,380 --> 00:35:51,220
Better.

689
00:35:51,220 --> 00:35:54,180
Hello, I'm David Park, Solutions Engineer.

690
00:35:54,180 --> 00:35:57,740
I specialize in system integration and technical implementation.

691
00:35:57,740 --> 00:35:59,540
Short and targeted.

692
00:35:59,540 --> 00:36:02,740
Always ask yourself, is this relevant to the meeting?

693
00:36:02,740 --> 00:36:05,500
What about pronunciation problems with names?

694
00:36:05,500 --> 00:36:07,460
Many learners worry about that.

695
00:36:07,460 --> 00:36:08,460
Good point.

696
00:36:08,460 --> 00:36:10,460
You can help listeners by pacing.

697
00:36:10,460 --> 00:36:12,860
My name is Nguyen Tron.

698
00:36:12,860 --> 00:36:16,460
Nguyen is spelled N-G-U-Y-E-N.

699
00:36:16,460 --> 00:36:18,900
That's very helpful in international meetings.

700
00:36:18,900 --> 00:36:21,860
Yes, and it shows communication awareness.

701
00:36:21,860 --> 00:36:24,740
Did you ever include years of experience?

702
00:36:24,740 --> 00:36:27,180
Only if it adds credibility and relevance.

703
00:36:27,180 --> 00:36:32,800
For example, I'm a cybersecurity consultant with 10 years of incident response experience.

704
00:36:32,800 --> 00:36:34,740
That works in expert meetings.

705
00:36:34,740 --> 00:36:35,740
Correct.

706
00:36:35,740 --> 00:36:38,100
Not necessary in routine team calls.

707
00:36:38,100 --> 00:36:40,740
Let's talk about client-facing introductions.

708
00:36:40,740 --> 00:36:42,380
These feel higher pressure.

709
00:36:42,380 --> 00:36:44,860
They are, because you represent the company.

710
00:36:44,860 --> 00:36:47,100
Use a value-focused statement.

711
00:36:47,100 --> 00:36:48,100
Example?

712
00:36:48,100 --> 00:36:49,100
Good morning.

713
00:36:49,100 --> 00:36:51,700
I'm Elena Ruiz, client success manager.

714
00:36:51,700 --> 00:36:56,140
I'll be your primary contact and will ensure your rollout runs smoothly.

715
00:36:56,140 --> 00:36:58,620
That immediately reassures the client.

716
00:36:58,620 --> 00:37:00,220
Exactly.

717
00:37:00,220 --> 00:37:02,620
Introductions should reduce client anxiety.

718
00:37:02,620 --> 00:37:05,420
What phrases show responsibility clearly?

719
00:37:05,420 --> 00:37:06,660
I'll be leading.

720
00:37:06,660 --> 00:37:07,900
I'll be supporting.

721
00:37:07,900 --> 00:37:09,340
I'll be coordinating.

722
00:37:09,340 --> 00:37:11,200
I'll be overseeing.

723
00:37:11,200 --> 00:37:12,700
Very useful verbs.

724
00:37:12,700 --> 00:37:15,220
Let's practice several fast examples.

725
00:37:15,220 --> 00:37:16,220
Go ahead.

726
00:37:16,220 --> 00:37:17,220
I'm Tom.

727
00:37:17,220 --> 00:37:18,580
I do marketing stuff.

728
00:37:18,580 --> 00:37:19,580
Upgrade.

729
00:37:19,580 --> 00:37:21,860
I'm Tom Walker, marketing manager.

730
00:37:21,860 --> 00:37:24,980
I lead campaign strategy and brand partnerships.

731
00:37:24,980 --> 00:37:26,220
I'm in IT.

732
00:37:26,220 --> 00:37:27,180
Upgrade.

733
00:37:27,180 --> 00:37:30,180
I'm Priyanka Das from IT Infrastructure.

734
00:37:30,180 --> 00:37:33,340
I manage network reliability and system security.

735
00:37:33,340 --> 00:37:34,700
Big improvement.

736
00:37:34,700 --> 00:37:37,540
Specific nouns increase perceived competence.

737
00:37:37,540 --> 00:37:40,020
What about when you are a guest in the meeting?

738
00:37:40,020 --> 00:37:42,100
Then acknowledge invitation.

739
00:37:42,100 --> 00:37:43,380
Thank you for having me.

740
00:37:43,380 --> 00:37:46,220
I'm Jordan Blake, external compliance advisor,

741
00:37:46,220 --> 00:37:48,580
here to review regulatory alignment.

742
00:37:48,580 --> 00:37:50,540
That sounds very professional.

743
00:37:50,540 --> 00:37:52,980
Gratitude plus purpose works well.

744
00:37:52,980 --> 00:37:55,500
Some meetings go around the table quickly.

745
00:37:55,500 --> 00:37:57,180
How do you stay concise?

746
00:37:57,180 --> 00:37:59,260
Use the 12 second model.

747
00:37:59,260 --> 00:38:03,140
Name, role, one responsibility line, stop.

748
00:38:03,140 --> 00:38:05,380
Don't keep talking unless prompted.

749
00:38:05,380 --> 00:38:06,220
Exactly.

750
00:38:06,220 --> 00:38:08,220
Silence is better than rambling.

751
00:38:08,220 --> 00:38:10,420
Let's discuss confidence language.

752
00:38:10,420 --> 00:38:12,460
What should your voice sound like?

753
00:38:12,460 --> 00:38:16,980
Moderate pace, medium volume, downward tone at sentence end.

754
00:38:16,980 --> 00:38:18,540
That signals certainty.

755
00:38:18,540 --> 00:38:20,940
Not rising tone like a question.

756
00:38:20,940 --> 00:38:21,780
Correct.

757
00:38:21,780 --> 00:38:23,500
Avoid sounding unsure.

758
00:38:23,500 --> 00:38:26,140
Should you smile while introducing yourself?

759
00:38:26,140 --> 00:38:30,060
Yes, it affects vocal tone and warmth even on audio calls.

760
00:38:30,060 --> 00:38:33,500
Let's talk about body language in in-person meetings.

761
00:38:33,500 --> 00:38:37,460
Sit upright, shoulders open, brief eye contact with the group,

762
00:38:37,460 --> 00:38:38,940
not just one person.

763
00:38:38,940 --> 00:38:40,780
What about hand gestures?

764
00:38:40,780 --> 00:38:43,580
Minimal and controlled, no fidgeting.

765
00:38:43,580 --> 00:38:47,100
Many learners also ask about humor in introductions.

766
00:38:47,100 --> 00:38:53,020
risky. Only use light humor if culture and context support it. Otherwise, skip it.

767
00:38:53,020 --> 00:38:54,140
Good advice.

768
00:38:54,140 --> 00:38:59,180
Now let's practice a full round table simulation. I'll prompt you. Please introduce yourself.

769
00:38:59,740 --> 00:39:05,180
Good afternoon. My name is Laura Chen. I'm a logistics coordinator and I manage shipment

770
00:39:05,180 --> 00:39:10,220
scheduling and vendor communication for this project. Looking forward to working with you all.

771
00:39:10,220 --> 00:39:14,140
Excellent. Clear and relevant. Try a senior version.

772
00:39:14,140 --> 00:39:17,900
Good afternoon. I'm Laura Chen, Head of Logistics.

773
00:39:17,900 --> 00:39:21,740
I oversee distribution strategy and international routing.

774
00:39:21,740 --> 00:39:25,740
Strong executive tone. Try a junior employee version.

775
00:39:25,740 --> 00:39:30,780
Hello, I'm Kevin Patel. I'm a junior data analyst and I support dashboard

776
00:39:30,780 --> 00:39:35,500
reporting and data validation. That sounds confident without exaggeration.

777
00:39:35,500 --> 00:39:38,780
Accuracy is critical. Never inflate your title.

778
00:39:38,780 --> 00:39:43,020
Yes, that can damage trust quickly. Let's cover a special case.

779
00:39:43,020 --> 00:39:44,620
when you're replacing someone.

780
00:39:44,620 --> 00:39:46,700
That happens often in projects.

781
00:39:46,700 --> 00:39:47,860
Example.

782
00:39:47,860 --> 00:39:49,820
Hello, I'm Nina Roce.

783
00:39:49,820 --> 00:39:52,060
I'll be taking over the account management role

784
00:39:52,060 --> 00:39:54,420
and will be your new contact going forward.

785
00:39:54,420 --> 00:39:56,420
Smooth transition language.

786
00:39:56,420 --> 00:39:58,900
Exactly. It reduces uncertainty.

787
00:39:58,900 --> 00:40:01,860
What if you didn't understand someone's introduction?

788
00:40:01,860 --> 00:40:03,100
Ask politely.

789
00:40:03,100 --> 00:40:04,740
Could you please repeat your role?

790
00:40:04,740 --> 00:40:08,340
Or, sorry, could you clarify your responsibility area?

791
00:40:08,340 --> 00:40:10,100
That's better than pretending.

792
00:40:10,100 --> 00:40:11,260
Always better.

793
00:40:11,260 --> 00:40:14,060
Let's talk about common mistakes learners make.

794
00:40:14,060 --> 00:40:19,460
To vague, to long, to quiet, to personal or no role clarity.

795
00:40:19,460 --> 00:40:21,760
Also apologizing too much.

796
00:40:21,760 --> 00:40:23,660
Yes, never start with,

797
00:40:23,660 --> 00:40:25,360
sorry, my English is bad.

798
00:40:25,360 --> 00:40:26,760
Start with your name.

799
00:40:26,760 --> 00:40:28,360
Confidence first.

800
00:40:28,360 --> 00:40:32,060
Language ability is judged after clarity, not before.

801
00:40:32,060 --> 00:40:34,260
Let's practice correction drills.

802
00:40:34,260 --> 00:40:36,260
I'll give bad, you fix.

803
00:40:36,260 --> 00:40:37,060
Ready.

804
00:40:37,060 --> 00:40:39,860
Myself, Raj, working here only.

805
00:40:39,860 --> 00:40:45,220
My name is Raj Mehta. I work in quality assurance and I handle product testing.

806
00:40:45,220 --> 00:40:47,380
I am here for support things.

807
00:40:47,380 --> 00:40:51,220
I'm here to support the implementation process and technical onboarding.

808
00:40:51,220 --> 00:40:52,740
Much more professional.

809
00:40:52,740 --> 00:40:55,860
Precision vocabulary matters in business settings.

810
00:40:55,860 --> 00:40:58,740
Should people prepare introductions in advance?

811
00:40:58,740 --> 00:41:01,460
Always. Treat it like a standard script.

812
00:41:01,460 --> 00:41:03,860
Even experienced managers do that.

813
00:41:03,860 --> 00:41:07,380
Yes, preparation reduces cognitive load under pressure.

814
00:41:07,380 --> 00:41:10,540
Let's give listeners a repeatable master template.

815
00:41:10,540 --> 00:41:14,940
Say, good morning slash afternoon, my name is blank.

816
00:41:14,940 --> 00:41:16,460
I'm the blank.

817
00:41:16,460 --> 00:41:18,500
I'm responsible for blank.

818
00:41:18,500 --> 00:41:21,780
That alone will solve most introduction problems.

819
00:41:21,780 --> 00:41:22,660
Agreed.

820
00:41:22,660 --> 00:41:26,380
Let's do several final model examples for different roles.

821
00:41:26,380 --> 00:41:29,620
Hello, I'm Victor Alvarez, financial controller.

822
00:41:29,620 --> 00:41:32,460
I supervise budgeting and cost analysis.

823
00:41:32,460 --> 00:41:36,340
Good morning, I'm Sophie Dubois, HR business partner.

824
00:41:36,340 --> 00:41:39,580
I support workforce planning and policy guidance.

825
00:41:39,580 --> 00:41:42,260
Hi, I'm Mark Jensen, product owner.

826
00:41:42,260 --> 00:41:45,460
I define feature requirements and delivery priorities.

827
00:41:45,460 --> 00:41:46,580
Good afternoon.

828
00:41:46,580 --> 00:41:49,300
I'm Lena Kowalski, legal counsel.

829
00:41:49,300 --> 00:41:52,220
I advise on contracts and compliance risk.

830
00:41:52,220 --> 00:41:54,420
Those are all clean and effective.

831
00:41:54,420 --> 00:41:58,460
Listeners should practice saying theirs out loud multiple times.

832
00:41:58,460 --> 00:42:00,900
Yes, spoken rehearsal is essential.

833
00:42:00,900 --> 00:42:02,780
Silent reading is not enough.

834
00:42:02,780 --> 00:42:07,260
Before we close, let's give one full natural meeting flow example.

835
00:42:07,260 --> 00:42:12,780
Good idea. Good morning, everyone. My name is Daniel Reed. I'm a Senior Project Manager

836
00:42:12,780 --> 00:42:17,020
and I'll be leading delivery coordination and stakeholder reporting for this engagement.

837
00:42:17,020 --> 00:42:21,980
Looking forward to working with you all. Excellent. Professional, warm, and complete.

838
00:42:21,980 --> 00:42:24,380
That final line, looking forward to working with you.

839
00:42:26,640 --> 00:42:30,160
Hello and welcome to Business English Conversations.

840
00:42:30,160 --> 00:42:33,200
Hi, everyone. We're so glad you're here with us today.

841
00:42:33,200 --> 00:42:39,120
I'm Erica. And I'm Marco. On this podcast, we help you improve your professional English through

842
00:42:39,120 --> 00:42:44,400
realistic workplace dialogues. If you enjoy learning with us, don't forget to follow the

843
00:42:44,400 --> 00:42:49,360
podcast and share it with someone who might find it helpful. Thank you so much for joining us.

844
00:42:49,920 --> 00:42:54,720
Today, we're going to talk about a topic that almost everyone faces at work.

845
00:42:54,720 --> 00:43:00,400
That's right. Today's conversation is about how to introduce yourself in a business meeting.

846
00:43:00,400 --> 00:43:04,800
a simple situation, but for many people, not so simple.

847
00:43:04,800 --> 00:43:09,200
Exactly. Introductions can feel surprisingly stressful.

848
00:43:09,200 --> 00:43:13,440
Especially when the meeting is formal or when you're speaking in English.

849
00:43:13,440 --> 00:43:15,680
Or when you're new to the company.

850
00:43:15,680 --> 00:43:17,760
Or meeting senior managers.

851
00:43:17,760 --> 00:43:19,840
Or important clients.

852
00:43:19,840 --> 00:43:24,080
So in today's episode, we're going to walk through this topic naturally,

853
00:43:24,080 --> 00:43:26,240
just as a flowing conversation.

854
00:43:26,240 --> 00:43:28,000
No rigid scripts.

855
00:43:28,000 --> 00:43:29,760
No complicated theory.

856
00:43:29,760 --> 00:43:33,760
Just practical, realistic English you can actually use.

857
00:43:33,760 --> 00:43:36,560
So, speaker one, let me ask you something.

858
00:43:36,560 --> 00:43:37,460
Go ahead.

859
00:43:37,460 --> 00:43:41,360
Do you remember the last time you had to introduce yourself in a meeting?

860
00:43:41,360 --> 00:43:43,460
Oh yes, very clearly.

861
00:43:43,460 --> 00:43:44,360
Nervous?

862
00:43:44,360 --> 00:43:47,360
A little, even though I've done it many times.

863
00:43:47,360 --> 00:43:48,860
That's comforting to hear.

864
00:43:48,860 --> 00:43:49,660
Why?

865
00:43:49,660 --> 00:43:53,560
Because many learners think nervousness means lack of ability.

866
00:43:53,560 --> 00:43:56,360
Not at all. Nervousness is human.

867
00:43:56,360 --> 00:43:59,460
Exactly. Even experienced professionals feel it.

868
00:43:59,460 --> 00:44:01,860
especially in new environments.

869
00:44:01,860 --> 00:44:03,660
Or high stakes meetings.

870
00:44:03,660 --> 00:44:06,360
Yes, context matters a lot.

871
00:44:06,360 --> 00:44:08,960
So what do you think people worry about most

872
00:44:08,960 --> 00:44:10,660
when introducing themselves?

873
00:44:10,660 --> 00:44:13,160
Usually, what should I say?

874
00:44:13,160 --> 00:44:14,760
The classic question.

875
00:44:14,760 --> 00:44:16,260
How long should it be?

876
00:44:16,260 --> 00:44:17,960
How formal should I sound?

877
00:44:17,960 --> 00:44:20,360
How do I avoid sounding awkward?

878
00:44:20,360 --> 00:44:21,860
Or unconfident.

879
00:44:21,860 --> 00:44:23,260
Let's start simple.

880
00:44:23,260 --> 00:44:25,960
What is the purpose of a business introduction?

881
00:44:25,960 --> 00:44:27,060
Good question.

882
00:44:27,060 --> 00:44:30,500
It's to clearly tell others who you are and what you do.

883
00:44:30,500 --> 00:44:33,300
Exactly. Identity and relevance.

884
00:44:33,300 --> 00:44:35,460
Not your entire biography.

885
00:44:35,460 --> 00:44:36,820
Definitely not.

886
00:44:36,820 --> 00:44:38,980
Meetings are about efficiency.

887
00:44:38,980 --> 00:44:40,180
And focus.

888
00:44:40,180 --> 00:44:43,060
So a good introduction should be concise.

889
00:44:43,060 --> 00:44:43,860
Clear.

890
00:44:43,860 --> 00:44:45,140
And professional.

891
00:44:45,140 --> 00:44:47,780
Let's imagine a typical situation.

892
00:44:47,780 --> 00:44:48,580
Okay.

893
00:44:48,580 --> 00:44:51,860
You're attending your first team meeting at a new job.

894
00:44:51,860 --> 00:44:53,220
The manager says,

895
00:44:53,220 --> 00:44:55,780
let's quickly go around and introduce ourselves.

896
00:44:55,780 --> 00:44:57,780
Your heart rate increases.

897
00:44:57,780 --> 00:44:59,780
You start rehearsing in your head.

898
00:44:59,780 --> 00:45:00,780
Name?

899
00:45:00,780 --> 00:45:01,780
Role?

900
00:45:01,780 --> 00:45:02,780
What else?

901
00:45:02,780 --> 00:45:04,780
Should I mention my experience?

902
00:45:04,780 --> 00:45:05,780
Should I keep it short?

903
00:45:05,780 --> 00:45:08,780
So, let's model a natural introduction.

904
00:45:08,780 --> 00:45:09,780
Go ahead.

905
00:45:09,780 --> 00:45:10,780
Good morning, everyone.

906
00:45:10,780 --> 00:45:11,780
My name is Marco.

907
00:45:11,780 --> 00:45:15,780
I've recently joined the sales team as an account executive.

908
00:45:15,780 --> 00:45:17,780
I'll be working mainly with regional clients.

909
00:45:17,780 --> 00:45:19,780
I'm really happy to be here.

910
00:45:19,780 --> 00:45:20,780
That sounds excellent.

911
00:45:20,780 --> 00:45:21,780
Why?

912
00:45:21,780 --> 00:45:23,780
It's structured but natural.

913
00:45:23,780 --> 00:45:24,780
Not robotic.

914
00:45:24,780 --> 00:45:32,340
robotic. You greeted the group, identified yourself, stated your position, mentioned your focus,

915
00:45:32,340 --> 00:45:38,740
and ended warmly. Let me try one. Please. Hello everyone. I'm Erica. I work in the operations

916
00:45:38,740 --> 00:45:44,700
department as a project coordinator. I'll be supporting workflow planning and team communication.

917
00:45:44,700 --> 00:45:50,420
Nice to meet you all. Perfectly balanced. Notice something important? What's that?

918
00:45:50,420 --> 00:45:56,060
introduction was too long. Yes, brevity is appreciated in meetings. Let's talk

919
00:45:56,060 --> 00:46:02,060
about confidence. Very important. Many people ask, how can I sound confident?

920
00:46:02,060 --> 00:46:07,520
Confidence in English is not about using complex vocabulary or sounding

921
00:46:07,520 --> 00:46:13,580
overly formal. It's about clarity, steady pacing, and tone. Speaking too

922
00:46:13,580 --> 00:46:19,300
fast often signals nervousness. Speaking too sloughly may signal uncertainty.

923
00:46:19,300 --> 00:46:22,540
So, aim for calm and clear.

924
00:46:22,540 --> 00:46:24,860
Another common issue is apologizing.

925
00:46:24,860 --> 00:46:26,380
Ah, yes.

926
00:46:26,380 --> 00:46:29,820
Sorry, my English is not very good.

927
00:46:29,820 --> 00:46:31,300
Understandable but risky.

928
00:46:31,300 --> 00:46:33,780
It can reduce your perceived confidence.

929
00:46:33,780 --> 00:46:36,320
Instead, just speak naturally.

930
00:46:36,320 --> 00:46:38,460
Most people are supportive anyway.

931
00:46:38,460 --> 00:46:39,460
Absolutely.

932
00:46:39,460 --> 00:46:41,480
Let's discuss formality.

933
00:46:41,480 --> 00:46:42,480
Good topic.

934
00:46:42,480 --> 00:46:45,540
How do you decide between high and good morning?

935
00:46:45,540 --> 00:46:48,020
It depends on the meeting culture.

936
00:46:48,020 --> 00:46:49,020
Industry.

937
00:46:49,020 --> 00:46:55,260
style, seniority of attendees, when unsure, start slightly more formal.

938
00:46:55,260 --> 00:46:57,380
You can always relax later.

939
00:46:57,380 --> 00:47:00,860
But it's harder to recover from being too casual.

940
00:47:00,860 --> 00:47:01,860
Very true.

941
00:47:01,860 --> 00:47:03,380
Let's compare examples.

942
00:47:03,380 --> 00:47:04,380
Okay.

943
00:47:04,380 --> 00:47:05,540
Hi, I'm Erica.

944
00:47:05,540 --> 00:47:06,540
Casual.

945
00:47:06,540 --> 00:47:07,540
Good morning.

946
00:47:07,540 --> 00:47:08,780
My name is Erica.

947
00:47:08,780 --> 00:47:09,940
More formal.

948
00:47:09,940 --> 00:47:12,340
Both correct, just different tones.

949
00:47:12,340 --> 00:47:14,260
Let's talk about common mistakes.

950
00:47:14,260 --> 00:47:16,340
Yes, very useful.

951
00:47:16,340 --> 00:47:18,980
Mistake one, too much personal information.

952
00:47:18,980 --> 00:47:23,340
I am 29 years old, I live in London, I love hiking.

953
00:47:23,340 --> 00:47:25,620
Not appropriate for most meetings.

954
00:47:25,620 --> 00:47:27,420
Mistake two, being too vague.

955
00:47:27,420 --> 00:47:28,980
Hi, I'm Marco.

956
00:47:28,980 --> 00:47:30,140
I work here.

957
00:47:30,140 --> 00:47:32,220
That leaves people confused.

958
00:47:32,220 --> 00:47:34,620
Mistake three, sounding memorized.

959
00:47:34,620 --> 00:47:38,220
Hello, my name is I am very happy.

960
00:47:38,220 --> 00:47:41,180
Grammatically correct, but unnatural.

961
00:47:41,180 --> 00:47:43,020
Natural rhythm matters.

962
00:47:43,020 --> 00:47:44,740
Let's discuss nervousness.

963
00:47:44,740 --> 00:47:46,700
a very real challenge.

964
00:47:46,700 --> 00:47:48,140
What if you freeze?

965
00:47:48,140 --> 00:47:49,860
First, breathe.

966
00:47:49,860 --> 00:47:51,580
Simple, but powerful.

967
00:47:51,580 --> 00:47:53,660
Second, slow down.

968
00:47:53,660 --> 00:47:56,060
Speed increases anxiety.

969
00:47:56,060 --> 00:47:58,940
Third, rely on a basic structure.

970
00:47:58,940 --> 00:48:01,820
Name, role, responsibility.

971
00:48:01,820 --> 00:48:03,320
Friendly close.

972
00:48:03,320 --> 00:48:05,380
Let's model a nervous version.

973
00:48:05,380 --> 00:48:06,220
Okay.

974
00:48:06,220 --> 00:48:09,100
Um, hello, my name is...

975
00:48:09,100 --> 00:48:10,740
Now, improved.

976
00:48:10,740 --> 00:48:12,660
Good morning, my name is Marco.

977
00:48:12,660 --> 00:48:16,100
I've just joined the HR team as a recruitment coordinator.

978
00:48:16,100 --> 00:48:17,300
I'm glad to be here.

979
00:48:17,300 --> 00:48:18,740
Much smoother.

980
00:48:18,740 --> 00:48:21,060
Let's explore virtual meetings.

981
00:48:21,060 --> 00:48:22,740
Increasingly common.

982
00:48:22,740 --> 00:48:26,100
Introductions online require extra clarity.

983
00:48:26,100 --> 00:48:28,380
Because body language is limited.

984
00:48:28,380 --> 00:48:29,340
Example.

985
00:48:29,340 --> 00:48:30,340
Hello everyone.

986
00:48:30,340 --> 00:48:31,460
I'm Erica.

987
00:48:31,460 --> 00:48:33,780
I'm joining from our Manchester office.

988
00:48:33,780 --> 00:48:36,020
I work as a business analyst focusing

989
00:48:36,020 --> 00:48:37,700
on process improvement.

990
00:48:37,700 --> 00:48:38,780
Great to be here.

991
00:48:38,780 --> 00:48:40,340
That works beautifully.

992
00:48:40,340 --> 00:48:41,180
Here's another.

993
00:48:41,180 --> 00:48:42,300
Here's another.

994
00:48:42,300 --> 00:48:44,260
Hi everyone, I'm Marco.

995
00:48:44,260 --> 00:48:47,780
I've recently joined the design team as a UX designer.

996
00:48:47,780 --> 00:48:50,980
I'll be working on user research and interface design.

997
00:48:50,980 --> 00:48:52,180
Nice to meet you all.

998
00:48:52,180 --> 00:48:53,820
Clear and friendly.

999
00:48:53,820 --> 00:48:56,340
Let's discuss seniority differences.

1000
00:48:56,340 --> 00:48:57,260
Yes.

1001
00:48:57,260 --> 00:48:59,220
A senior executive might say,

1002
00:48:59,220 --> 00:49:02,780
Good morning, I'm Erica, director of operations.

1003
00:49:02,780 --> 00:49:06,500
I oversee strategic planning and performance management.

1004
00:49:06,500 --> 00:49:08,500
Authoritative and concise.

1005
00:49:08,500 --> 00:49:10,260
A junior employee.

1006
00:49:10,260 --> 00:49:12,140
Good morning, my name is Marco.

1007
00:49:12,140 --> 00:49:14,500
I'm a graduate trainee in finance.

1008
00:49:14,500 --> 00:49:16,660
I'm here to support the reporting team.

1009
00:49:16,660 --> 00:49:20,340
Both appropriate, just different levels of responsibility.

1010
00:49:20,340 --> 00:49:22,500
Let's discuss when someone asks,

1011
00:49:22,500 --> 00:49:24,140
tell us a bit about yourself.

1012
00:49:24,140 --> 00:49:26,740
That question scares many people

1013
00:49:26,740 --> 00:49:29,300
because a bit is vague.

1014
00:49:29,300 --> 00:49:31,660
Let's model a balanced response.

1015
00:49:31,660 --> 00:49:33,380
Sure, I'm Erica.

1016
00:49:33,380 --> 00:49:36,500
I work as a project coordinator in operations.

1017
00:49:36,500 --> 00:49:39,820
My background is logistics and workflow planning.

1018
00:49:39,820 --> 00:49:43,460
Recently, I've been focusing on cross-team communication.

1019
00:49:43,460 --> 00:49:44,400
Excellent.

1020
00:49:44,400 --> 00:49:45,360
Here's another.

1021
00:49:45,360 --> 00:49:46,300
I'm Marco.

1022
00:49:46,300 --> 00:49:49,220
I'm part of the marketing team as a brand executive.

1023
00:49:49,220 --> 00:49:51,500
I've worked mainly on digital campaigns

1024
00:49:51,500 --> 00:49:53,660
and customer engagement strategies.

1025
00:49:53,660 --> 00:49:55,540
Natural and relevant.

1026
00:49:55,540 --> 00:49:58,300
Let's talk about friendliness and introductions.

1027
00:49:58,300 --> 00:50:00,940
Yes, business doesn't mean cold.

1028
00:50:00,940 --> 00:50:03,060
Warmth helps build connection.

1029
00:50:03,060 --> 00:50:04,140
For example?

1030
00:50:04,140 --> 00:50:05,820
It's great to meet you all.

1031
00:50:05,820 --> 00:50:07,900
I'm happy to be part of this meeting.

1032
00:50:07,900 --> 00:50:10,260
I look forward to working with everyone.

1033
00:50:10,260 --> 00:50:12,780
These phrases humanize your introduction

1034
00:50:12,780 --> 00:50:14,800
without sounding unprofessional.

1035
00:50:14,800 --> 00:50:17,540
Let's imagine a cross-departmental meeting.

1036
00:50:17,540 --> 00:50:19,460
People from different teams.

1037
00:50:19,460 --> 00:50:21,540
You might say, hello everyone,

1038
00:50:21,540 --> 00:50:23,660
I'm Marco from the IT department.

1039
00:50:23,660 --> 00:50:25,460
I work as a systems engineer,

1040
00:50:25,460 --> 00:50:28,500
focusing on infrastructure and network support.

1041
00:50:28,500 --> 00:50:29,700
Very clear.

1042
00:50:29,700 --> 00:50:32,620
Or good afternoon, my name is Erica.

1043
00:50:32,620 --> 00:50:34,860
I represent the customer service team.

1044
00:50:34,860 --> 00:50:37,900
I handle client support and issue resolution.

1045
00:50:37,900 --> 00:50:38,900
Excellent.

1046
00:50:38,900 --> 00:50:40,520
Let's talk about names.

1047
00:50:40,520 --> 00:50:41,940
Important detail.

1048
00:50:41,940 --> 00:50:44,020
Speak your name clearly.

1049
00:50:44,020 --> 00:50:45,260
Not rushed.

1050
00:50:45,260 --> 00:50:47,340
If your name is often mispronounced,

1051
00:50:47,340 --> 00:50:48,980
you can gently help.

1052
00:50:48,980 --> 00:50:50,420
My name isn't Gwynn.

1053
00:50:50,420 --> 00:50:52,060
That's pronounced when.

1054
00:50:52,060 --> 00:50:54,260
Very professional and helpful.

1055
00:50:54,260 --> 00:50:56,020
Let's discuss energy.

1056
00:50:56,020 --> 00:50:58,980
Yes, energy influences perception.

1057
00:50:58,980 --> 00:51:01,580
To flat may sound disengaged.

1058
00:51:01,580 --> 00:51:04,580
To enthusiastic may sound unnatural.

1059
00:51:04,580 --> 00:51:06,940
Aim for warm professionalism.

1060
00:51:06,940 --> 00:51:08,420
Let's try examples.

1061
00:51:08,420 --> 00:51:09,860
Hi, I'm Marco.

1062
00:51:09,860 --> 00:51:10,740
Better.

1063
00:51:10,740 --> 00:51:11,580
Hi, everyone.

1064
00:51:11,580 --> 00:51:12,420
I'm Marco.

1065
00:51:12,420 --> 00:51:13,500
Nice to meet you.

1066
00:51:13,500 --> 00:51:15,220
Subtle but effective.

1067
00:51:15,220 --> 00:51:18,300
Let's explore introductions when joining late.

1068
00:51:18,300 --> 00:51:19,420
Good scenario.

1069
00:51:19,420 --> 00:51:20,740
Sorry for joining late.

1070
00:51:20,740 --> 00:51:22,380
I'm Erica from Finance.

1071
00:51:22,380 --> 00:51:23,660
Thank you for waiting.

1072
00:51:23,660 --> 00:51:25,140
Polite and smooth.

1073
00:51:25,140 --> 00:51:27,460
Let's talk about memory pressure.

1074
00:51:27,460 --> 00:51:28,620
Ah, yes.

1075
00:51:28,620 --> 00:51:31,660
When many people introduce themselves before you.

1076
00:51:31,660 --> 00:51:34,140
And you try to remember all names.

1077
00:51:34,140 --> 00:51:36,060
which is nearly impossible.

1078
00:51:36,060 --> 00:51:39,300
Perfectly okay to focus on your own introduction

1079
00:51:39,300 --> 00:51:41,280
and learn names gradually.

1080
00:51:41,280 --> 00:51:43,660
Let's discuss cultural differences.

1081
00:51:43,660 --> 00:51:45,080
Very important.

1082
00:51:45,080 --> 00:51:48,380
Some cultures prefer very brief introductions.

1083
00:51:48,380 --> 00:51:50,860
Others expect slightly more detail.

1084
00:51:50,860 --> 00:51:52,720
Observing others helps.

1085
00:51:52,720 --> 00:51:55,580
Adaptability is a key professional skill.

1086
00:51:55,580 --> 00:51:58,700
Let's model a confident, polished introduction.

1087
00:51:58,700 --> 00:51:59,860
Good morning, everyone.

1088
00:51:59,860 --> 00:52:01,340
My name is Marco.

1089
00:52:01,340 --> 00:52:03,540
I'm a senior consultant specializing

1090
00:52:03,540 --> 00:52:05,460
in organizational strategy.

1091
00:52:05,460 --> 00:52:08,260
I'll be supporting this project from the planning phase.

1092
00:52:08,260 --> 00:52:09,900
It's a pleasure to be here.

1093
00:52:09,900 --> 00:52:12,220
Strong, clear, and professional.

1094
00:52:12,220 --> 00:52:14,340
Let's do another, more relaxed.

1095
00:52:14,340 --> 00:52:17,740
Hi everyone, I'm Erica from Product Development.

1096
00:52:17,740 --> 00:52:21,460
I work as a design engineer focusing on new prototypes.

1097
00:52:21,460 --> 00:52:22,660
Great to meet you all.

1098
00:52:22,660 --> 00:52:24,220
Friendly and natural.

1099
00:52:24,220 --> 00:52:26,420
So what's the key takeaway?

1100
00:52:26,420 --> 00:52:27,500
Keep it clear.

1101
00:52:27,500 --> 00:52:28,860
Keep it relevant.

1102
00:52:28,860 --> 00:52:30,220
Keep it concise.

1103
00:52:30,220 --> 00:52:32,940
And speak with calm confidence.

1104
00:52:32,940 --> 00:52:35,980
Remember, introductions are not performances.

1105
00:52:35,980 --> 00:52:38,500
They're simply professional courtesies

1106
00:52:38,500 --> 00:52:41,100
and opportunities to create connection.

1107
00:52:41,100 --> 00:52:43,980
Well, that was a very practical discussion.

1108
00:52:43,980 --> 00:52:46,540
Yes, and an essential workplace skill.

1109
00:52:46,540 --> 00:52:49,460
We hope this conversation helps you feel more confident

1110
00:52:49,460 --> 00:52:50,620
in your next meeting.

1111
00:52:50,620 --> 00:52:51,820
Before we finish,

1112
00:52:51,820 --> 00:52:54,660
let's leave you with a small reflection question.

1113
00:52:54,660 --> 00:52:57,740
Imagine you're entering a business meeting tomorrow.

1114
00:52:57,740 --> 00:52:59,460
How would you introduce yourself?

1115
00:52:59,460 --> 00:53:01,180
Try saying it out loud.

1116
00:53:01,180 --> 00:53:02,920
Practice makes confidence.

1117
00:53:02,920 --> 00:53:07,120
Thank you so much for listening to Business English Conversations.

1118
00:53:07,120 --> 00:53:11,480
Keep practicing, keep improving, and keep showing up with confidence.

1119
00:53:11,480 --> 00:53:13,000
See you in the next episode.

1120
00:53:13,000 --> 00:53:14,000
Goodbye.

1121
00:53:14,000 --> 00:53:14,400
Goodbye, everyone.
